tind-wireguard
==============

What it does
  Gives every Tind device an always-on, split-tunnel WireGuard connection to the
  servr.no server. Only the server's own subnet and names under servr.no use the
  tunnel; everything else (including Proton VPN) is unaffected.

Per-device steps
  1. sudo tind-enroll init            -> prints this device's PUBLIC key
  2. send the public key to the server admin; the admin replies with an enrolment block
     (ADDRESS, SERVER_ENDPOINT, SERVER_PUBLIC_KEY, ALLOWED_IPS, DNS_SERVER)
  3. sudo tind-enroll apply           -> paste the block, end with Ctrl-D (or: apply FILE)
  4. tind-enroll status

Design rules
  - Each device makes its own key. No private key is ever in the Tind image.
  - The image contains no server details at all: no endpoint, no server key, no subnet.
    They arrive in the enrolment block, so nothing about the server is in the repository.
  - The block is parsed as data, never run as shell; every value is checked before use.
  - ALLOWED_IPS must never contain 0.0.0.0/0 (tind-enroll refuses).
  - Send the block over a channel you trust: whoever controls DNS_SERVER controls what
    names under servr.no resolve to while the tunnel is up.
  - Server side: docs/server/tind-add-peer and docs/servr-wireguard-server.md in the Tind repository.
